<%# Getting started. Deliberately honest about difficulty rather than
    promising one magic command — the mission argument says adoption beats
    elegance, which means telling people the truth about the evening ahead
    and then making it as short as possible. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    You need a computer that stays on, half an hour, and a willingness to
    paste two or three commands into a terminal. That is genuinely the bar
    today — not because it should be, but because it is, and we would
    rather you know before you start than halfway through.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no account to create, no signup, no key to request. You end
    the evening with a web address on your home network that your household
    logs into.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Three ways in</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Docker", subtitle: "The usual answer") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Build the image and run it. One command each, and the container
        carries everything the app needs — no Ruby to install, no
        dependencies to chase. If you already run anything else at home this
        way, this will take you ten minutes.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "From the source", subtitle: "If you'd rather see it") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Clone the repository, install the dependencies, prepare the
        database, start the server. Four commands, all of them the standard
        ones for this kind of application, and the whole thing is readable
        while it runs.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "A small server", subtitle: "If you want it always on") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The repository carries deployment configuration for putting it on a
        machine you rent or one in your cupboard. Worth doing once you know
        you want to keep it, not on the first evening.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
  <p class="mt-6 text-soft">
    The exact commands live in the repository's README, where they can stay
    correct as the app changes — a version of them copied onto this page
    would be wrong within a month.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What happens the first time you open it</h2>
  <ol class="mt-6 space-y-4">
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">1 · You make the household</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        The first visit to a fresh installation is a single form: name your
        household, make your own account. That account is the admin, because
        somebody has to be, and it is you because you got there first.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">2 · You choose what you want</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        A short walkthrough asks which parts of life you want the app to
        cover. Everything is switchable later, and switching a part off
        hides it rather than losing anything.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">3 · You invite the household</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        Invitations are links you copy and hand over however you like — a
        message, a note on the table. There is no mail server to configure
        because nothing here needs to send you email.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">4 · You point it at a model, when you're ready</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        Optional, and worth leaving until you've had a look around. The app
        is a complete organiser without it, and every page that needs a
        model says so plainly rather than failing oddly.
        <%= link_to "How the model works →", about_page_path("models"), class: "text-primary-700 hover:underline" %>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Keeping it</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Your entire installation is a database file and a folder of
    attachments. Backing up means copying those two things somewhere else —
    an external drive, another machine, wherever you already put things you
    would hate to lose. Restoring means copying them back. There is no
    export format to fight with, because there is no lock-in to escape.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Do set that up. Self-hosting means the failure that loses your data is
    now your hardware, and a household calendar is worth ten minutes of
    thinking about backups.
  </p>
</section>
